> planner/base.py
> """
> Provider-neutral model adapter protocol.
> 
> Adapters encapsulate provider SDKs and expose only capabilities required by
> Planner/Responder. They do not choose plans, mutate learning state, or inject
> planner-owned context.
> 
> Required adapter contract:
> 
> class ModelAdapter(Protocol):
>     def chat_response(
>         self,
>         messages: Sequence[Mapping[str, Any]],
>         max_tokens: int | None = None,
>     ) -> str: ...
> 
>     def stream_response(
>         self,
>         messages: Sequence[Mapping[str, Any]],
>         max_tokens: int | None = None,
>     ) -> Iterator[str]: ...
> 
>     def allowed(self, tokens: int) -> bool:
>         '''
>         Return False when unavailable due to context limits, quota, health,
>         policy, provider outage, or other execution constraints.
>         '''
>         ...
> 
>     def estimate_cost(
>         self,
>         prompt_tokens: int,
>         completion_tokens: int,
>     ) -> float | None: ...
> 
> Caller contract:
> - responders.py calls `adapter.allowed(estimated_prompt_tokens)` before
>   invocation;
> - responders.py calls chat_response/stream_response only after Planner's
>   context assembler has created effective messages;
> - metrics.py records actual provider usage where available;
> - no adapter should rely on a global mutable "last responder".
> """
> 
> =====
> 
> We are currently implementing a general LLM-authored JavaScript planning/execution system in an existing Python `planner/` package.
> 
> Goal
> Implement a general LLM-authored JavaScript planning/execution system in the existing Python planner/ package.
> 
> An LLM may return visible user-facing text plus an optional fenced plan:
> 
> async function main(input) {
>   // restricted async JavaScript
> }
> The JavaScript program is the plan. It may call registered tools and use ordinary bounded control flow, including loops, conditionals, functions, and concurrent fan-out/fan-in.
> 
> We explicitly do not create task-specific plan classes such as PeopleDossierPlan.
> 
> Dynamic and Recursive Planning
> A plan may call the registered llm_text tool. That call can return ordinary text, structured data, or a new fenced plan block.
> 
> A returned plan is a child execution, not a rewrite of the parent program:
> 
> The parent can have multiple outstanding llm_text calls, each potentially producing an independent child plan.
> Child plans inherit a bounded portion of the parent’s execution budget and receive explicit input/context derived from the calling result.
> Child plans may themselves call llm_text and create further children, subject to maximum depth, operation, tool-cost, and wall-time budgets.
> The trace records parent/child execution IDs and logical call locations.
> Dynamic plan output is returned to the calling JavaScript as structured data, including visible text, final result, status, and child trace reference.
> This supports adaptive research: an LLM reviewing poor search results can issue a different retrieval strategy, use different tools, or synthesize directly.
> 
> Retry
> retry is no longer central to the design. Dynamic replanning is generally preferable when recovery requires judgment or a changed strategy.
> 
> We may retain:
> 
> await retry({ attempts: 1..N }, async attempt => ...)
> as a narrowly defined convenience for transient mechanical failures. It retries only callbacks that throw and is always bounded. It is not a substitute for LLM-directed recovery.
> 
> JavaScript must run only in a sandboxed subprocess/runtime—preferably QuickJS or Deno with no permissions—not Node vm. Python remains the security and policy boundary.
> 
> Registry-Derived Tool Interface
> Do not hard-code a JS_API dictionary.
> 
> javascript_tools(registry) derives the exposed manifest from Registry.list_tools().
> javascript_prompt(registry) describes those tools and JS helpers.
> JavaScript function names resolve to pinned registered tools.
> Tool outputs are bounded JSON-safe data or artifact references; tool-originated text is untrusted data, never executable instructions.
> llm_text is a normal registered tool. If its invocation specifies a model, use it; otherwise route through the Planner’s ordinary registered-model selection logic.
> 
> Execution and Attribution
> Every run produces a comprehensive execution record, including:
> 
